{"body":"> Besides that, if I understood you correctly, you recommend introducing a `new` constructor that also performs some validation instead of instantiating like this:\n>\n> ```rust\n> self.artifacts.entry(cid).or_insert_with(|| Artifact {\n>                     name,\n>                     locations: HashMap::new(),\n>                     attestations: BTreeSet::new(),\n>                 });\n> ```\n>\n> Two questions:\n> - Is there any prior art on handling and bubbling up errors when applying actions to a COB? \n\nSo, I think in fact, it might even be better to create a type\n`Description` that does the validation.\n\nNow, the trick is that you want to protect the construction of that,\nand remember that `Deserialize` is also a way of constructing one of\nthese.\nSo you would want to introduce a `Description::new` method that\nreturns an error for validation failures.\nThis `Description::new` can be used in `TryFrom<&str>`,\n`TryFrom<String>`, and `FromStr` implementations.\nYou can then do something like this:\n```rust\n#[derive(Deserialize, Serialize)]\n#[serde(try_from = \"String\", into = \"String\")]\npub struct Description {\n    inner: String,\n}\n\nimpl From<Description> for String {\n    fn from(Description { inner }: Description) -> Self {\n        inner\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWith that, you could keep the `enum` as is, changing the `String` for `Description`.\n\nThose using the Rust API need to construct a `Description`, those\nusing JSON need to go through `Deserialize` – which is also how\n`radicle` loads actions.\n\nTo be honest, I would still recommend hiding your actions away from\nthe `pub enum`, and having constructors for them. You'll save yourself\npotential headaches when you need to evolved the actions by adding\nfields, modifying fields, etc. You want to control that as much as\npossible, without leaking what's inside.\n\n> - Do we have apply limits on strings elsewhere so that I can go based on the same baseline?\n\nNot really, unfortunately. I think the issue and patch stuff are free\nform. I made an effort to keep the job COB small by keeping values\nsmall and repeatable, e.g. the status value.","replyTo":"bdcd0995adb95a43879036554e61c4e785d283eb","type":"comment"}